---

<DocsLayout
  title="Terminal-control"
  subtitle="@nikcli-ai/terminal-control records, replays and bundles PTY-driven TUI sessions."
  toc={toc}
  description="The @nikcli-ai/terminal-control package: native termctrl driver, .termctrl recordings, and PR-ready evidence bundles."
>
  <section id="overview" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      <code>@nikcli-ai/terminal-control</code> is the recording-and-evidence engine for
      PTY-driven TUI sessions. It wraps a native <code>termctrl</code> driver that captures
      every frame of a terminal session into a <code>.termctrl</code> recording and lets
      you render that recording as MP4, GIF preview, single-frame PNG, SVG, ANSI-coloured
      text, JSON, or PR-ready Markdown.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is the third sibling engine — alongside
      <a href="/docs/browser-control">@nikcli-ai/browser-control</a> (headless Chromium) and
      <a href="/docs/computer-use">@nikcli-ai/computer-use</a> (real desktops) — that shares
      the same evidence-bundle output format. New agents only learn one bundle shape, and
      PR bots can treat all three sibling engines the same way.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Native binary">
      Unlike the other two siblings, terminal-control ships a native <code>termctrl</code>
      driver in <code>target/</code>. The <code>--binary FILE</code> flag lets you point the
      CLI at a custom build; the default is the one bundled in the package.
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="vs-browser" class="doc-section">
    <h2>How it differs from browser-control</h2>
    <p>
      Browser-control and computer-use ship their own daemon that holds sessions alive
      across CLI invocations. Terminal-control is different: every command except
      <code>bundle</code> and <code>install-skill</code> is forwarded to the native
      <code>termctrl</code> driver. Run <code>terminal-control COMMAND --help</code> for
      the native command surface.
    </p>
    <p>
      What the JS package owns is the recording format (<code>.termctrl</code>), the
      renderer set (<code>json</code>, <code>text</code>, <code>ansi</code>,
      <code>png</code>, <code>svg</code>, <code>video</code>), and the
      <a href="#bundle"><code>bundle</code></a> command that turns a recording into a
      PR-ready artifact set.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cli" class="doc-section">
    <h2>CLI surface</h2>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Terminal">
      # Register the bundled skill in WORKSPACE/.agents/skills so nikcli discovers it.
      # WORKSPACE defaults to the nearest Git worktree. Idempotent relative symlink.
      terminal-control install-skill [--workspace DIR] [--json]

      # Per-command help is forwarded to the native termctrl driver:
      terminal-control COMMAND --help
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      The <code>--json</code> flag on <code>install-skill</code> returns a typed payload
      describing the install (source, target, installed boolean) — useful for test harnesses
      and for the <code>nikcli</code> install pipeline.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="bundle" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Bundle command</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>bundle</code> command is the package's headline feature: it takes a
      <code>.termctrl</code> recording and produces a directory containing a screenshot, a
      video, an inline GIF preview, a <code>manifest.json</code>, and a
      <code>pr.md</code> ready to be appended to a pull request description.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Terminal">
      terminal-control bundle \
        --recording ./session.termctrl \
        --out ./artifacts/terminal \
        --link-base artifacts/terminal \
        --result passed \
        --title "TUI verification" \
        --summary "The terminal session completed successfully." \
        --at-marker before-quit \
        --fps 20
    </DocCode>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Flag</th>
          <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--recording FILE</code></td>
          <td>Required input — a <code>.termctrl</code> recording produced by the native driver.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--out DIR</code></td>
          <td>Output directory for <code>screenshot.png</code>, <code>demo.mp4</code>, <code>preview.gif</code>, <code>manifest.json</code>, and <code>pr.md</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--result passed|failed|unverified</code></td>
          <td>Verification status baked into the manifest and the PR section.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--title TEXT</code> / <code>--summary TEXT</code></td>
          <td>Customise the PR section header and summary.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--link-base PATH</code></td>
          <td>Repository-relative artifact path used by <code>pr.md</code> when published.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--at-marker NAME</code></td>
          <td>Capture the screenshot at a recording marker.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--at-ms MS</code></td>
          <td>Capture the screenshot nearest this timestamp.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--edit FILE</code></td>
          <td>Marker-based termctrl video edit plan.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--binary FILE</code></td>
          <td>Explicit <code>termctrl</code> binary (default: bundled in <code>target/</code>).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--fps NUMBER</code></td>
          <td>Video frames per second (default: <code>20</code>).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--tail-ms MS</code></td>
          <td>Final-frame hold (default: <code>1000</code>).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--include-recording</code></td>
          <td>Copy the sensitive raw recording into the bundle.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--no-video</code></td>
          <td>Produce only screen evidence (skip the MP4).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--no-preview</code></td>
          <td>Skip the inline GIF preview.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--no-footer</code></td>
          <td>Omit the video footer overlay.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--show-cursor</code></td>
          <td>Keep the terminal cursor visible in the video.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--json</code></td>
          <td>Print the resulting bundle as JSON instead of writing files.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="success" title="Identical bundle shape">
      The bundle format matches <a href="/docs/browser-control">browser-control</a> and
      <a href="/docs/computer-use">computer-use</a>: <code>screenshot.png</code>,
      <code>demo.mp4</code>, <code>preview.gif</code>, <code>manifest.json</code>,
      <code>pr.md</code>. PR pipelines and <code>nikcli</code>'s own bundle command treat
      all three siblings uniformly.
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="agent" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Agent integration</h2>
    <p>
      Terminal recordings are usually produced by test harnesses and CI workflows rather
      than by agents at runtime, but the package is the canonical way to render any
      recorded TUI session for PR evidence or a docs site. The <code>--json</code> flag
      is the integration point for test harnesses that want to inspect the bundle
      programmatically.
    </p>
    <div class="doc-grid not-prose">
      <DocCard title="Renderers" eyebrow="src/render/">
        <p>
          <code>json.ts</code>, <code>text.ts</code>, <code>ansi.ts</code>,
          <code>png.ts</code>, <code>svg.ts</code>, <code>video.ts</code>. The renderer
          set handles both colour and monochrome terminals, with optional cursor
          visibility and a configurable final-frame hold.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Recording format" eyebrow="src/recording.ts">
        <p>
          <code>RECORDING_VERSION</code> tracks the versioned <code>.termctrl</code>
          container. <code>duration</code>, <code>frameAt</code>, <code>finalFrame</code>,
          and <code>frameAtMarker</code> read it back.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Evidence bundle" eyebrow="src/evidence.ts">
        <p>
          Same shape as the sibling engines: <code>createEvidenceBundle</code> writes
          <code>screenshot.png</code>, <code>demo.mp4</code>, <code>preview.gif</code>,
          <code>manifest.json</code>, and <code>pr.md</code> into <code>--out DIR</code>.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
    </div>
  </section>

